Z||000000||Ed||10-16-2000||08:32 PM||||The Sacramento Fiero Club (Northern Calif. Chapter, FOCOA) joined forces with the San Francisco Club (Golden Gate Fieros) for a "Pumpkin Tour" of Sonoma County, north of San Francisco this last Saturday.  <P>My wife and I continued on to Bodega Bay on the north coast.<P>I spend many an idle hour wondering if I'd drive back roads any faster in a Porsche, or a Ferrari Daytona like we saw in Bodega, or in a Lamborghini 350 GT like we saw at Tomales Bay. <P>Every time I get out on the open road, I wonder, "Would this be any more fun in a ...?"  I doubt it.  I think I go as fast as I would in any of these others.  I know I'm not at the limit of the Fiero.  <P>So, as I deliberate whether I need the '88 upgrade, or Corvette brakes, I ask myself, "Would this really allow me to go faster, or am I doing this for some other reason?"  (Which is OK too, just different.)<P>Bottom line:  The Fiero has a really high fun factor, and is a real bargain in the GT/Sports Car world.    <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-16-2000||09:25 PM||||Ed, you have discovered what I found, the Fiero is a fun car to drive.  I take some back roads when heading west and the slight rolls, twists and bends in the road are fun when at not the high rate of speed. 70 mph in a Fiero is just as fast as 70 mph in a Porsche and you haven't spent as much $.<P>Upgrades, sure, when certain components wear out why not if you like the car.  The Corvette brakes may stop you better.  Which isn't such a bad idea afterall.<P>It sounds like you had an enjoyable weekend.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Robert||10-18-2000||12:31 AM||||Say Ed:<BR>Glad you had a great trip, but this is the first I have heard of it.  And I am a member of the Golden Gate Fieros.  Just wondering how the word was passed around?<P> I might have enjoyed the trip also, but my stock brakes would really be smoking in short order.  Upgrading my breaks is on my “short list” of projects.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> ||||reg||5
Z||000003||Ed||10-18-2000||02:25 PM||||I'm not sure how word got passed on to Golden Gate, but there were two members from the Bay area, and one from Napa who belongs to both clubs. <P>I think there may have been some "word of mouth" involved, especially since two of our members, past officers, live north of San Francisco and often pass the word along to Golden Gate.<P>I'm new to the presidency of the Sacramento Club, so I'm still getting organized.  When I am better organized then maybe I'll be better about letting Golden Gate know when we're doing something in your area.<P>I'll try to keep that in mind.<p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
